Why a licence file in a private repository at all — `repository-baseline-policy`
tier C does not require one: absence of a licence and "all rights reserved" are
the same legal position but not the same signal. An unlicensed tree reads as an
oversight, and the day this code is read outside the org — an audit, a diligence
request, the spin-out of the generic execution framework that
`alpha-engine-config-I10647` names as a deferred option — the question gets asked
at the worst possible moment.

The public half of this system is `nousergon/crucible`, licensed
AGPL-3.0-or-later. Nothing here is a copy of it: the coupling is the two contract
documents, and `crucible` is consumed as a pinned dependency.
